  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough next Wednesday...\n\nActive weather is expected over the next 7 days. It starts Friday\nas anomalously high temperatures in the 80s F and minimum relative\nhumidity values in the single digits to low teens combine to \nbring elevated fire weather conditions across Southeastern \nMontana. Winds are expected to gust into at least the 20s mph \nacross the region with winds increasing to the west. Mountain and \nfoothills locations are expected to see winds gusting into the 30s\nand 40s mph. Stronger winds and lower September precipitation \nvalues for these locations means critical fire weather conditions \nare expected for the foothills west of Billings and Sheridan \nCounty Wyoming.\n\nStarting Saturday, upper level troughing drops into the Pacific\nnorthwest bringing a pattern change to cooler and wetter\nconditions. A cold front moves through Saturday bringing Pacific\nmoisture and breezy north/northeast winds. Differences in frontal\ntiming will lead to a significant temperature gradient with\nlocations near Judith gap likely seeing a high temperature in the\n50s F while locations in far Southeast Montana will get into the \n70s F. Precipitation is likely to move into the western mountains\nand foothills Saturday before spreading across the region \nSaturday night through Monday. There are still many factors \ninfluencing the forecast, the biggest of which is Hurricane Rachel\nwhich is expected to weaken to a tropical storm over the next few\ndays, but still remain a tropical depression into Wyoming. Rachel\nwill bring an injection of moisture, which is the main reason \nprecipitation amounts continue to increase, especially east of \nRosebud County. Probabilities for greater than 1 inch of \nprecipitation are 50-100% west of Rosebud County, with the \nhighest values in the mountains and foothills. Chances are 20-40% \nfor Rosebud County and east, with the lowest values being near the\nWyoming border. Precipitation type will be tricky with this \nforecast, as most locations will likely see a mix of rain and \nsnow. Heavy snow is likely in the Crazy/Beartooth mountains, but \nis much more uncertain elsewhere. The chance for greater than 6 \ninches of snow for the foothills west of Billings remains low \n(<20%).\n\nHigh temperatures Sunday and Monday will be in the 40s F for most\nwith lows in the 20s and 30s F Sunday through Tuesday mornings.\nEnsemble systems are indicating that the middle of next week will\nsee temperatures increase back to seasonable values in the 50s and\n60s F. There are also indications that another large upper level\nwave may impact the region, though this is highly uncertain at\nthis time. Torgerson",
